EXT. FROZEN LAKE - ICE-FISHING SHACK - DAY

A tin shack on a plain of white. Wind pushes snow across the lake. A thin plume of smoke rises from the stovepipe.

INT. ICE-FISHING SHACK - CONTINUOUS

Cramped. A bunk, a wood stove, two holes in the floor with lines dangling into black water. Four people live here this week.

GRETA HOLLINGSWORTH, forties, sits by the stove, sorting matches. LEIF AASEN, twenties, hunches over a hole with a rod. DANTE FIORENTINO, fifties, lies on the bunk with a blanket up to his chin. They have not spoken in an hour.

LEIF
Anything?

GRETA
Not yet.

DANTE
It'll bite when the barometer drops.

LEIF
We don't have a barometer.

DANTE
Then it'll bite when it feels like it.

Greta tosses a match into the stove. It flares, spits, goes out.

GRETA
Forty hours. We said we'd be home in twelve.

LEIF
We said the truck would start.

DANTE
The truck started.

GRETA
It started and then it died, Dante.

DANTE
It died in a way that was consistent with starting.

Leif laughs despite himself. Greta almost does. The line in the hole twitches. Leif grabs the rod. Greta rises. Dante sits up.

LEIF
I've got something.

GRETA
Easy. Easy.

DANTE
Don't horse it.

Leif reels. The water breaks. A pike, long as his arm, thrashes up through the hole and flops onto the floor boards, where it lies still, mouth working.

All three stare at it. The shack is silent.

GRETA
Well.

Then the shack door bangs open. A STRANGER fills the doorway, fifties, bundled head to boot in a snow-caked parka, ice in his beard, a snowmobile helmet under one arm.

STRANGER
I smelled the smoke from two miles away. Anybody home?

LEIF
Mister, you have no idea.

DANTE
Close the door. You're letting out the warm.

STRANGER
Sorry. Sorry.

He shuts it. Greta stares at him. He stares at the pike.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
That your supper?

GRETA
It's our supper if you've got a sled.

STRANGER
Got a sled. Got a tow rope. Got a thermos of coffee, too.

DANTE
Coffee.

LEIF
Coffee!

GRETA
(over them)
Sit down, mister--

TOBIAS
Wren. Tobias Wren.

DANTE
(over Greta)
Wren, there's a sled?

LEIF
(over both)
Is it big enough for four?

TOBIAS
It's big enough for four and a fish.

Greta sits him on the bunk and presses a tin mug into his hands. The pike gives one last flop.
